  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does a charge controller do?",
      "a": "It controls the current flowing from the panels into the batteries, so they charge fully without being overcharged. Better ones, called MPPT controllers, also get the most power out of the panels.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an MPPT controller?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an off-grid system need batteries?",
      "a": "The sun shines only by day and varies with the weather, but people want light at night. Batteries store spare daytime energy so it can be used later.",
      "asks": [
        "Can solar panels work without batteries?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does an off-grid home need an inverter?",
      "a": "Panels and batteries give direct current, but most household appliances are made for alternating current. The inverter converts it, though small systems can run some lights and phone chargers straight from direct current.",
      "asks": [
        "Do I need an inverter for solar panels?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you size an off-grid solar system?",
      "a": "Add up the energy the home uses each day in kilowatt-hours, then choose enough battery to cover a few cloudy days and enough panels to recharge it, allowing for short winter days.",
      "asks": [
        "How many solar panels do I need?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who uses off-grid solar?",
      "a": "Millions of homes in rural Africa and Asia use solar home systems, often just a panel, a battery and LED lights, because the grid does not reach them. Cabins, boats and camper vans use them too.",
      "asks": [
        "Where is off-grid solar used?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Solar panels and batteries both work with direct current, so an inverter is needed for ordinary household appliances.",
    "Solar home systems bring electricity to millions of homes not connected to a grid, especially in Africa and South Asia."
  ],
